WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
Tor is strongest when the threat is a network observer trying to connect a user's original network address with a destination.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:14.680
On public Wi-Fi, the network operator may see that Tor is being used, but it should not see the websites inside the Tor circuit.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:20.460
An internet provider may log a connection to Tor, but not the final site reached through an ordinary circuit.

4
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:28.000
A destination website may see a Tor exit address, but not the home, office, or school address that would appear in a direct connection.

5
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.400
Tor can also help with access barriers.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:40.620
If a website is blocked by a local network or a national filtering system, Tor may provide a path to reach information that would otherwise be unavailable.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:47.360
In places where using Tor directly is blocked, bridges and other access methods can help some users reach the network.

8
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:54.420
This is still about privacy and access, not a guarantee that every block can be bypassed or that every situation has the same risk.

9
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.980
A useful way to test any claim about Tor is to name the observer.

10
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.640
Against a hotel Wi-Fi operator, Tor may hide the final news site.

11
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:08.820
Against the news site itself, Tor may hide the user's original network address but not the pages requested.

12
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.360
Against an account provider, Tor may hide the connection source but not the account name.

13
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:21.100
Against malware on the device, Tor may provide little help because the exposure happens before traffic leaves the computer.

14
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:26.420
Tor does not protect against everything a website learns from what you do on the site.

15
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:37.800
If you log into a personal email account, post under a known username, submit a phone number, or reuse a writing style that clearly identifies you, Tor cannot make that information anonymous.

16
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:43.260
The website may not know your original network address, but it can still know the account and content you gave it.

17
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.880
Identity leaks often come from behavior rather than routing.

18
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.720
Tor also does not make downloaded files safe.

19
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:57.260
A document, media file, or installer may contain active content, metadata, tracking links, or malware.

20
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:06.880
Opening a downloaded file outside Tor Browser can connect through the normal network path or reveal local details, depending on the file and application.

21
00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:16.820
The safer habit is to avoid unnecessary downloads during sensitive sessions and to handle files in a controlled environment when there is a legitimate reason to inspect them.

22
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.900
Tor does not defeat endpoint compromise.

23
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:27.440
If the device is already monitored by malware, a hostile administrator, or unsafe software, the network route may not matter.

24
00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:35.940
Screen capture, clipboard monitoring, keystroke logging, and browser exploitation can expose information before it ever enters the Tor circuit.

25
00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:45.080
Tor is one layer in a security plan; it is not a substitute for a maintained operating system, trusted software, and sensible device hygiene.

26
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.200
A clear threat model makes these limits manageable.

27
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:55.860
Ask what you are trying to protect, from whom, for how long, and what failure would look like.

28
00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:02.520
Tor may be very helpful against a local network observer and less helpful against a service where you identify yourself.

29
00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.820
It may help read public information privately but not protect a file opened in a normal app.

30
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.920
The most responsible Tor users are not the most dramatic; they are the ones who match the tool to the risk.
